ing an idle channel. If, during this backoff period, another device starts transmitting, the current device will defer its transmission.\n\nFurthermore, to enhance the effectiveness of collision avoidance, IEEE 802.11 often incorporates acknowledgments (ACKs). After a device transmits data, it expects to receive an acknowledgment from the receiving device. If an ACK is not received within a specified timeframe, the transmitting device assumes that the transmission was lost due to a collision or interference and will retransmit the data. This mechanism, combined with the initial channel sensing and random backoff, forms the core of how IEEE 802.11 manages shared wireless resources, ensuring that multiple devices can communicate without excessive data loss and with reasonable performance."}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 200, "completion_tokens": 356, "total_tokens": 556, "compute_duration_seconds": 2.165483236312866, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}